            "chapter_title": "\"Facts & Parties – Background of the Litigation\"",
            "description": "",
            "`Facts`: In August 2020 Bruce Cohen sued his former employer Consilio LLC seeking unpaid overtime wages and statutory penalties under Minnesota law.\n\n `Parties`: Consilio is a nationwide legal document review firm operating offices in Minnesota;\nbut Mr Cohen was hired only in Minnesota as an hourly licensed attorney document reviewer.\n\n`Key alleged violations`: Ms Cohen claimed violations under three statutes – the Minnesota Payment Of Wages Act (MPWA), the Minnesota Fair Labor Standards Act (MFLSA) ‑ often called MFA — *and* the Minnesota Wage Theft Act (MWTA).\n\n`Settlement stipulations`: prior to any land based discovery both parties confirmed\nthat Consilio had paid Mr Cohen all wages he claimed were owed ($256 000+)\nand his liquidated damages ($462).\n\n`Remaining dispute`: Whether Mr Cohen was entitled to additional *penalties* (“average daily wage” under MPWA or other statutorily sanctioned sanctions) was left unresolved.\n\n`Class dimension`: The complaint was filed *on behalf* of Mr Cohen *and…* a putative class.\n\n**Subtopics**:\n•Consilio’s business model\n•Mr Cohen’s employment status\n•Premium‐overtime policy announcement\n•Removal of higher premium pay\n•Allegation details \n•Initial suit filing August 2020\n•Case classification status\n•Settlement payment terms\n•Stipulations confirming full payment\ny •Solv → Dispute remaining over punitive money"
